# Draft Tack Work Item

Draft a Tack ticket with the Tack MCP. Read
`tack_getting_started` once per session before any other Tack
tool, then use the tools and inputs that guide names for each step below.
Show a draft first. Ask the user whether to create the ticket in Tack. Do not
create until the user says yes.

Use the Ask Questions tool at every decision point.

## Ticket prose

Write the name and description to the `make-readable` standard.

- Put the main point first.
- Use short complete sentences with concrete subjects and verbs.
- Every sentence needs a subject and a verb.
- Do not use fragments or telegraphic shorthand.
- Remove narration, filler, repetition, and unasked recommendations.
- Keep only details the assignee needs to do the work.
- Use bullets only when bullets make the description easier to scan.
- If a bullet is a sentence, it must still be a complete sentence.

**Bad** (fragments):

> Short terse. Saying something.

**Good** (short but complete):

> Coordinate with Acme Inc. to verify the Accounting events populate in relative order.

Brief means fewer sentences. Brief does not mean broken sentences.

## Links

Every ticket must include relevant links.

External URLs go in the issue description. Parent epics use the parent or
epic scope input from `tack_getting_started`. Related Tack tickets use the
relationship tool from that guide after creation. Do not embed relationship
metadata in the description body.

Example:

> Move both `rate_limit.v1.yaml` and `rate_limit.v2.yaml` from `gateway/` to
> `edge_config/` per Alex's request. Remove the v1 file after the freeze ends.
>
> Design doc: [link]
> Related PR: [link]

Do not create a ticket without at least one supporting link unless the user
opts out.

| Link type | Where it goes |
| --- | --- |
| Slack, PR, Figma, Datadog, Confluence | Description on create |
| Epic | Parent or epic scope on create |
| Blocks, relates, duplicates, other Tack tickets | Relationship tool after creation |

**Bad** (relationship metadata in the body):

```
Epic: XYZ
Relates: ZYA
Blocks: ABC
```

**Good** (ticket reference in prose when it reads naturally):

> After the work in PROJ-65, migrate the remaining rate limit files from
> `gateway/` to `edge_config/`.

## Description shape

Body structure and detail level are guidelines. They are not strict templates.

Use context to choose the shape of the description. Look at the conversation,
sibling tickets, the type of work, and how much orientation the assignee needs.
The examples below are common patterns. Adapt them or combine them when the
situation calls for something different.

## Task (default)

A Task describes concrete work for an engineer.

The task may name systems, repos, and files. Do not prescribe exact
implementation steps.

Match the `[Prefix]` naming convention from sibling tickets under the same
parent epic.

When work splits into multiple Tasks, ask which naming style fits.

**Repo-scoped** names work when the task maps to specific repos or layers:

- `[Prefix] [Web] Do X`
- `[Prefix] [API] Do Y`
- `[Prefix] [sample-web-assets] Add assets for Z`

**General** names work when the task is cross-cutting or not tied to one repo:

- `[Prefix] Do X`
- `[Prefix] Investigate Y`
- `[Prefix] Create Z in the docs site`

## Bug

A Bug report usually has three sections.

- **Steps to reproduce**
- **Observed behavior**
- **Expected behavior**

Do not add technical analysis, root cause guesses, or proposed fixes unless the
user asks for them.

A short Bug description may use one or two complete sentences plus an external
link for a known flaky test. A customer-facing defect may need a full multi-step
repro.

## Detail patterns

Pick a pattern from context. Patterns may blend when the work needs more than
one shape.

### Brief

Use a few complete sentences. Bullets are fine when each bullet is a full
sentence. This pattern fits small, obvious tasks.

Example:

> - Coordinate with Docs on the changelog event timing.
> - Verify the changelog event populates correctly before the freeze.

### Standard

Use a short paragraph of prose with external links. This pattern fits most work.

See the example in the Links section above.

### Detailed

Use sections such as Context and What needs to happen. This pattern fits
cross-team work or work that needs significant orientation. External links may
appear at the end of the description or inline where they read naturally.

Example:

> ## Context
> This work unblocks the rollout because the rate limit files still live under
> `gateway/`.
>
> ## What needs to happen
> 1. Move `rate_limit.v1.yaml` to `edge_config/`.
> 2. Move `rate_limit.v2.yaml` to `edge_config/`.
> 3. Remove the v1 file after the freeze ends.
>
> Design doc: [link]
> Related PR: [link]

## Rules

- Create an issue with the create tool from `tack_getting_started`. Tack does
  not use Jira issue types. Default the work framing to Task. For a Bug, keep
  the Bug description shape and ask whether to attach a `Bug` label with the
  relationship tool from that guide.
- Match sibling ticket naming under the parent epic. Tickets in the same
  initiative usually share a bracket prefix such as `[Feature Launch]`.
- Do not assign anyone unless the user provides a member. Assignment uses the
  relationship tool from `tack_getting_started` after creation.
- Pass the parent or epic scope when the request includes an epic. Otherwise
  pass exactly one scope input from `tack_getting_started`.
- Put the description body in properties under the description property name
  from the property-defs step in `tack_getting_started`.
- On tool errors, follow the response `Next step` line rather than retrying the
  same call. If create still fails, output the ticket details for manual
  creation.

## Flow

### Step 1: Mode and context

Ask the user:

- Task or Bug?
- Parent epic? Pre-fill from context when context already includes it.
- Project, if there is no epic?
- Related or blocking Tack tickets?

Resolve the workspace with the workspace list tool from `tack_getting_started`
(ask when more than one workspace is visible).

### Step 2: Description shape

Decide the description length, sections, and tone from context. Look at sibling
tickets and the detail patterns above. Ask the user only when the shape is
ambiguous.

### Step 3: Draft

Look up sibling tickets with the issue list tool from `tack_getting_started`
under the parent epic or project. Match their naming convention. Show the user:

- Name with naming prefix
- Description in `make-readable` prose with external links in the body
- Parent epic or other scope reference for create
- Tack ticket relationships to add after creation

### Step 4: Review draft

Ask whether the draft looks good or needs edits. Iterate until the user is
satisfied.

### Step 5: Ask to create

Ask whether the user wants to create the ticket in Tack.

If the user says no, stop and leave the draft for them to copy or edit.

If the user says yes, continue to Step 6.

### Step 6: Create

After the user says yes, create the issue with the create tool from
`tack_getting_started`. Pass workspace, name, description properties, and
parent or other scope using the inputs that guide defines.

### Step 7: Tack ticket relationships

Add each relationship from Step 1 with the relationship tool from
`tack_getting_started`.

### Step 8: Done

Return the printed reference from the create response.
